_printf \- produces output according to a format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "main.h"
.PP
.BI "int _printf(const char *" format ", ...);"
.SH DESCRIPTION
_printf produces output according to a format string.
It writes output to stdout, the standard output stream.
.PP
The format string is composed of zero or more directives.
When a % symbol is encountered, the next character determines
what value is printed. The following conversion specifiers are handled:
.TP
.B %c
Prints a single character.
.TP
.B %s
Prints a string of characters. If the string is NULL, prints (null).
.TP
.B %d
Prints a decimal integer.
.TP
.B %i
Prints an integer.
.TP
.B %%
Prints a literal percent sign.
.TP
.B unknown specifiers
If an unknown specifier is used such as %r, it is printed literally.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, returns the total number of characters printed,
excluding the null byte used to end output to strings.
If the format string is NULL, returns \-1.
